<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      Theme for RearActivity, taken from another rear-panel app that has never
      exhibited the failure this fixes.

      THE SYMPTOM: locking the device made the rear panel's override appear on the
      FRONT screen, over the keyguard.

      THE CAUSE was the previous theme, Theme.Material.NoActionBar.Fullscreen: an
      OPAQUE fullscreen activity. RearActivity holds FLAG_SHOW_WHEN_LOCKED (it must —
      that is what keeps the panel alive through a lock), and when the keyguard comes
      up the system looks for a top opaque fullscreen activity to show over it. An
      opaque activity is exactly that candidate; a translucent one is not, so it is
      never hoisted onto the primary display in the first place.

      windowAnimationStyle=@null matters for the same reason and is not cosmetic: the
      default window animations run this window through the system's lock/unlock
      transition, which is itself a path by which it gets drawn on display 0.
      RearActivity's overridePendingTransition(0, 0) only covers its own launch, not
      system-driven transitions.

      Translucency costs nothing visually here: RearDialView.onDraw opens with
      canvas.drawColor(Panel.Palette.BG), so the view paints its own opaque background
      across the whole surface. Nothing shows through.
    -->
    <style name="RearTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar.Fullscreen">
        <item name="android:windowAnimationStyle">@null</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowIsTranslucent">true</item>
    </style>
    <!--
      The pinned picture on the front (ui/PinViewActivity): see-through, so the app it opens over is there while the
      picture grows out of the back's place and the black comes up behind it, and again as it closes. No window animation
      of the system's (it draws its own open and close), no bars' colour, drawn under the cutout.
    -->
    <style name="PinViewTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar">
        <item name="android:windowAnimationStyle">@null</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowIsTranslucent">true</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:enforceStatusBarContrast">false</item>
        <item name="android:enforceNavigationBarContrast">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowLayoutInDisplayCutoutMode">always</item>
    </style>
    <!--
      Our camera page on the back (ui/camera/RearCameraActivity). OPAQUE AND BLACK, unlike RearTheme: the cover steps
      away the moment the page is on top of the back, and a see-through window would show Xiaomi's launcher under it
      until its first frame; an opaque one gets a black starting window at once. The system's empty splash (no icon:
      the app's icon is never shown on the back) is black too. It does not show over the lock screen (no
      showWhenLocked), so it is never the opaque candidate RearTheme's note warns of: it simply closes when the phone
      locks. No window animation: the moon is back at once when it closes.
    -->
    <style name="RearCameraTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.Material.NoActionBar.Fullscreen">
        <item name="android:windowAnimationStyle">@null</item>
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@android:color/black</item>
        <item name="android:windowIsTranslucent">false</item>
        <item name="android:windowLayoutInDisplayCutoutMode">always</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenBackground">@android:color/black</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/nothing</item>
        <item name="android:windowSplashScreenIconBackgroundColor">@android:color/black</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/black</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/black</item>
    </style>
</resources>
